.TH CCTRANSCRIPT 1 "2026-08-09" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cctranscript \- render a timestamped transcript of a Claude Code session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cctranscript
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B cctranscript \-\-list
.RI [ \-\-repo
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-all ]
.br
.B cctranscript \-\-search
.I term
.RI [ \-\-regex ]
.RI [ \-\-case ]
.RI [ \-\-repo
.IR name ]
.RI [ \-\-all ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cctranscript \-\-from\-archive
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ session ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Claude Code records each session as a raw JSON-lines log under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> .jsonl.
Every message in that log is timestamped, but the chat panel and the terminal
UI don't show per-message times.
.B cctranscript
reads the log and prints who said what and when \(em your prompts and Claude's
replies \(em with the elapsed delta between turns, so you can see where a
session actually spent its time.
.PP
The default view is human and iMessage-style: your prompts mirror to the right,
Claude's replies sit on the left, each tagged with the model that produced it
.RB ( "Opus 4.8" ", " "Sonnet 5" )
and coloured by model family, so a mid-session model switch is visible at a
glance. A full-width numbered rule opens each
.B exchange
\(em one of your prompts and every reply to it \(em so a long transcript scans
as sections. Each of Claude's text replies to prompt
.I N
carries a citable reference
.IR N . M
.RI ( M
resets per prompt); thinking and tool turns aren't numbered, since the citable
unit is what a human would quote.
.PP
By default the view is the conversation only \(em your prompts and Claude's
text replies. Thinking blocks, tool calls and their results are hidden until
you ask for them with
.BR \-\-think ", " \-\-tools " or " \-\-full ,
keeping the everyday transcript readable and revealing the machinery when you
need to debug a session.
.PP
With no
.I session
argument the tool renders the latest session for the repo you are currently in,
recovered from each log's recorded working directory rather than from Claude
Code's dash-mangled folder name (which can't be reversed). Pass a session UUID
(or a unique prefix), a path to a
.I .jsonl
log, or use
.BR \-\-repo " and " \-n
to reach back to an older session or one in another repo.
.PP
The summary line under the header also reports how big the context grew: the
largest single request the session sent, counting the cached prefix
.RB ( input_tokens " + " cache_creation_input_tokens " + " cache_read_input_tokens )
\(em cached blocks still occupy the window, so counting fresh input alone would
report a 400k conversation as a few hundred tokens. It is the peak rather than
the size at the end, so a compacted session still shows what it actually held.
.B \-\-json
carries both, as
.IR context.peak " and " context.final .
A log with no usage records (an older or synthetic one) simply omits the figure
instead of reporting a context of zero.
.PP
Subagents are reported as two figures, not one:
.B N agents started
and
.BR "N finished" .
.I Started
counts the spawn tool calls in the log, which is a ceiling \(em a spawn the
operator skipped, or one stopped mid-run, still counts.
.I Finished
counts the per-agent logs in the sibling
.I <session-uuid>/subagents/
directory, one written per agent that actually ran. The gap between them is the
point: it is where a spawn that never became a run shows up.
.B \-\-json
carries them as
.IR agents.started " and " agents.finished ,
with the spawn breakdown by subagent type as
.IR agents.byType .
.PP
Both figures work in
.B \-\-from\-archive
mode.
.BR ccarchive (1)
captures every
.I .jsonl
at any depth and mirrors it at the same relative path, so the per-agent logs are
in the archive too, just gzipped. The
.I .meta.json
sidecar beside each one is not captured, which is why the count keys on the logs
alone. Reading the directory is a name listing only \(em it never opens a log,
so it cannot fault an iCloud-evicted archive's bytes back down.
.PP
Neither figure bounds the other. An agent that spawns its own agent writes into
the principal's
.I subagents/
directory, while
.I started
sees only the principal's own spawn calls \(em so a nested run legitimately
finishes more agents than it started, and the figures are left unclamped rather
than hide that.
.PP
Where no per-agent store is reachable \(em an archive predating subagent
capture, a pruned store, a log copied away from its directory \(em
.I finished
is reported as
.BR "finished unknown" ,
and
.B \-\-json
gives
.I agents.finished
as
.B null
with
.I agents.finishedKnown
false. It is never zero: the key is always present, so an absent field can never
be mistaken for a count, and a consumer writing
.I agents.finished || 0
is choosing that reading rather than falling into it.
.PP
This reconciles two rules that pull against each other. Both chips print on
every run, including
.BR "0 agents started \(bu 0 finished" ,
because the summary line is read by comparing two sessions side by side and a
chip that vanishes leaves the reader working out which figure is missing from
which line. But a figure the log never recorded is unknown, and printing it as
zero would be a claim the evidence doesn't support. So the field
.I set
is fixed and the
.I value
carries the honesty: the chip holds its position and says
.BR unknown .
A zero, by contrast, is printed whenever it is a fact \(em with no spawn call in
the log, no agent can have run, so
.B 0 finished
is proved by the log itself even with no directory to read. (The context figure
is the case with no such pair to keep aligned, so it simply drops out instead.)
.SS Searching across sessions
Rendering answers
.I "what happened in this session".
.B \-\-search
answers the other question \(em
.I "which session said X"
\(em which is the one you actually have when you cannot remember the session.
It sweeps every session the scoping flags admit and prints an index of the turns
that match, grouped by session, most recent session first and chronological
within one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
a1b2c3d4  2026\-07\-26 14:22:07  [atelier]
   3.2  claude  \(embudgeted against the resolved width\(em
   7.1  claude  \(emone row per matching turn\(em   (2 hits)

4 hits in 2 sessions \(bu searched prompts+replies \(bu 440 of 440 sessions swept in 2.1 s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every row carries the citable
.IR N . M
reference for its turn, and that reference is
.IR gate-invariant :
only prompts and text replies are numbered, and both are collected whatever
.BR \-\-tools " or " \-\-think
say, so a ref printed by a search means the identical turn when you reopen the
session with default flags. A hit in a tool call or a tool result has no
reference of its own; it reports the number of the exchange it sits inside, plus
its layer, rather than a number that would not resolve.
.PP
One row per matching
.IR turn ,
not per match: a turn hit three times shows the first excerpt and a
.B (3 hits)
count, so the output stays proportional to turns rather than to how common the
term is. The excerpt is a single line, whitespace collapsed, centred on the
match and fitted to the width. That is unconditional and applies to every layer,
because no layer is safe to print whole \(em replies top out around 8 KB, but a
single
.I prompt
in the live store reaches 848 KB, since a pasted blob is a prompt.
.PP
The term is a
.B literal
by default: a path with a
.B .
in it must not silently become a wildcard.
.B \-\-regex
opts into a regular expression. Matching is
.B case-insensitive
by default \(em people search for
.IR worktree ", not " Worktree
\(em and
.B \-\-case
opts into sensitivity.
.PP
Prompts and replies are searched by default, and the gates that widen the render
widen the search identically:
.B \-\-tools
searches tool calls and their results too (in full, not the one-line summary the
render shows). The tool layers are 31% of the corpus and filenames live there,
so
a run that did
.I not
search them says so and says how many sessions hold the term there \(em the
boundary is reported as a count on every run, never as silence. There is no
default cap on the result set: a long output is honest, and a limit picked from
today's corpus size would be a fitted number.
.B \-\-top
truncates deliberately, and prints what it hid.
.PP
The sweep reads every candidate log, which is what
.B \-\-materialise
names: an iCloud-evicted archive mirror is skipped and counted by default, and
.B \-\-materialise
faults it back.
.PP
The live logs are ephemeral: Claude Code deletes them after
.I cleanupPeriodDays
(default 30). With
.B \-\-from\-archive
the tool reads the compressed mirror
.BR ccarchive (1)
maintains instead \(em the same discovery, listing and rendering, over
.IR <dest>/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> .jsonl.gz
\(em so a session the cleanup has already removed can still be listed and
rendered, word for word. The archive location resolves exactly as in
.BR ccarchive (1):
.BR \-\-dest ,
then
.BR $CCARCHIVE_DEST ,
then the iCloud Drive default. An explicit path to a
.I .jsonl.gz
file is read transparently with no flag at all.
.PP
Colour is automatic: on when writing to a terminal, off when piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.B \-\-json
emits a machine-readable array of turns \(em the same refs, roles, models,
timestamps and text \(em and is never styled, so it is safe to pipe into
.BR jq (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I session
A session UUID (or a unique prefix of one), a path to a
.IR .jsonl " or " .jsonl.gz
log, or omitted to use the latest session for the current repo.
.TP
.B \-\-from\-archive
Read from the
.BR ccarchive (1)
archive instead of the live logs. Every other option works unchanged.
Evicted (dataless) iCloud files are respected: listing never reads one \(em
its first-prompt column shows an
.B evicted
marker instead \(em but rendering a chosen session will fetch its bytes back,
which is the point of a deliberate read.
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " dir"
Where the archive lives (default: iCloud Drive, or
.BR $CCARCHIVE_DEST ).
Implies
.BR \-\-from\-archive .
.TP
.B \-\-materialise
With
.BR \-\-search ,
also read archive mirrors whose local bytes iCloud has evicted, faulting them
back down. Without it such mirrors are skipped and the count is printed in the
summary, so a skipped file is never silently absent from a result set. Same word
and same meaning as in
.BR ccarchive (1)
and
.BR ccrepo (1);
it applies to
.B \-\-search
alone, because search is this tool's only bulk read.
.TP
.BI \-\-repo " name"
Use sessions from the named repo \(em its folder basename, e.g.
.I atelier
\(em instead of the current directory.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the sessions for the repo (id, start time, first prompt) and exit, rather
than rendering a transcript. Combine with
.B \-\-repo
for another repo, or
.B \-\-all
for every repo.
.TP
.B \-\-all
With
.BR \-\-list " or " \-\-search ,
span every repo instead of the current one.
.TP
.BI \-\-search " term"
Search every session the scoping flags admit and print an index of the turns
that match, instead of rendering one transcript. Literal by default;
case-insensitive by default. All the scoping options above apply
.RB ( \-\-repo ", " \-\-all ", " \-\-from\-archive ", " \-\-dest ),
and a
.I session
argument narrows the sweep to that one session, which is how you grep inside a
transcript you already have. Passing
.B \-\-list
as well is harmless and ignored: a search
.I is
a list, narrowed.
.TP
.B \-\-regex
Treat the
.B \-\-search
term as a regular expression rather than a literal string.
.TP
.B \-\-case
Match case-sensitively. The default folds case.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " YYYYMMDD"
With
.BR \-\-search ,
include only hits on or after this local date \(em the same word, and the same
per-message meaning, as in
.BR ccrepo (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-until " YYYYMMDD"
With
.BR \-\-search ,
include only hits on or before this local date.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " n"
Keep at most
.I n
rows per level of the search index \(em at most
.I n
sessions, and at most
.I n
hits within each \(em after ordering, the same operation as in
.BR ccrepo (1).
What it hid is counted and printed. There is no default limit.
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-last " k"
Use the
.IR k -th
most recent session
.RI ( 1
= latest, the default) instead of the newest.
.TP
.B \-\-tools
Include tool calls and their results, each summarised to a single line.
.TP
.B \-\-think
Include Claude's thinking blocks.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Everything: prompts, replies, thinking and tools.
.TP
.B \-\-utc
Print timestamps in UTC. The default is your local timezone.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-delta
Hide the elapsed-time deltas shown between messages.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " n"
Wrap the body to
.I n
columns. The default is the terminal width, capped at 100.
.TP
.BR \-\-color ", " \-\-no\-color
Force ANSI colour on or off. The default is colour when writing to a terminal
and plain when piped or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set;
.B FORCE_COLOR
forces it on.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable array of turns instead of the human view, alongside the
session's
.I context
peak and final size and its
.I agents
started/finished pair. Under
.B \-\-search
it emits an array of
.I sessions
instead, each holding its
.I hits
.RI ( ref ", " role ", " timestamp ", " excerpt ", " hits ),
under a
.I meta
block naming the term, the layers searched, the sessions swept and matched, and
every skip count \(em zeros included, so two runs compare line for line. Never
styled; safe to pipe into
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise usage summary and exit. This manual is the full reference.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
Claude Code's raw session logs, one folder per repo. Read-only to this tool.
.TP
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> .jsonl
A single session log \(em the source
.B cctranscript
reads and renders.
.TP
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> /subagents/
One log per subagent that actually ran under that session. Listed by name only,
for the
.B finished
count; the logs themselves are never opened.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Mobile\ Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/cc-transcripts/
The default
.BR ccarchive (1)
archive
.B \-\-from\-archive
reads \(em the same mirror layout, gzip-compressed. Overridden by
.B \-\-dest
or
.BR $CCARCHIVE_DEST .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cctranscript
Render the latest session in this repo.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-full
The latest session with thinking and tool calls included.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-list
What sessions exist for this repo.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-list \-\-all
Every session across every repo.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-n 2
The second-most-recent session here.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-repo ros \-\-tools
The latest
.I ros
session, with tool calls.
.TP
.B cctranscript 1a4fa531\-...
A specific session by UUID (or a unique prefix).
.TP
.B cctranscript ./path/to/log.jsonl
A specific log file by path \(em a
.I .jsonl.gz
archive mirror works here too, no flag needed.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-from\-archive \-\-list
Every archived session for this repo, including ones Claude Code's cleanup has
already deleted from the live store.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-from\-archive 1a4fa531
Render an archived session by UUID prefix, straight from the mirror.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-json | jq .
The machine-readable form, for scripting.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search worktree
Which turns in this repo's sessions mention
.IR worktree .
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search \-\-all pointerscan
The same question across every repo.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search \-\-tools 'floor.py'
Include tool calls and results \(em where a filename usually lives.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search \-\-regex '\-\-materialis[ez]'
A pattern rather than a literal.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search Māori \-\-case
Case-sensitive, and non-ASCII terms match: the logs are read as UTF-8.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search leakscan \-\-since 20260801 \-\-until 20260809
Only hits inside a date window, judged on each turn's own timestamp.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search \-\-from\-archive \-\-all secretscan
Search the archive instead \(em including sessions the cleanup has deleted.
.TP
.B cctranscript \-\-search \-\-json ADR | jq '.meta'
What the sweep did: layers, counts and skips.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success, including
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-list ,
and a
.B \-\-search
that finds nothing (an empty result is an answer, not a failure).
.PP
.B 1
when the tool cannot select a session to render: no log at an explicit path, no
session matching a given UUID or prefix, no sessions for the named repo, no
Claude sessions found for the current directory, or a
.B \-n
index larger than the number of sessions available.
.PP
.B 2
on a bad argument:
.B \-\-search
with no term, an invalid
.B \-\-regex
pattern, a
.BR \-\-since / \-\-until
that is not a date, or a non-positive
.BR \-\-top .
.SH NOTES
The tool reads
.I ~/.claude/projects/
(or, with
.BR \-\-from\-archive ,
the archive directory) read-only and writes nothing \(em no state, no cache,
no output file.
.PP
On an iCloud-backed archive, macOS may have evicted a file's local bytes
("Optimise Mac Storage"). Listing skips reading such files so a
.B \-\-list
never bulk-downloads the archive; rendering one evicted session faults just
that file back from iCloud, deliberately.
.PP
.B \-\-think
cannot widen a search, because there is nothing to widen it to. Claude Code
stopped writing thinking text to the log: re-measured across the whole live
store on 2026-08-09, of
.B 31,800
thinking blocks exactly
.B 9
carry any text, all of them between 2026-06-05 and 2026-07-04, every block since
carrying a signature and no content. So
.B \-\-think
renders no thinking turns on a current session either, and
.B \-\-search
does not offer a thinking layer rather than offer one that is always empty.
.PP
.BR \-n / \-\-last
is ignored by
.BR \-\-search ,
exactly as it is already ignored by
.BR \-\-list .
Both modes return a
.IR set ,
not a pick, and making
.B \-n
mean "the k-th hit" would give one word two meanings.
.PP
A search reports the layer it did
.I not
read. Because the sweep must read every byte of every candidate anyway, testing
the unsearched layers costs nothing, so a run without
.B \-\-tools
always says how many sessions hold the term in their tool calls or results \(em
including when that is none. The count is stated rather than left to be
inferred.
.PP
A search is
.B gated
on each log's raw JSON text and only then decoded turn by turn, which is what
keeps a full sweep at the cost of a bare read. One consequence is worth knowing
for
.BR \-\-regex :
in the raw log a double quote, a backslash and a newline appear in their escaped
forms, so a pattern that must match one of those should be written against the
escaped form. A literal
.B \-\-search
term needs no such care \(em it is gated on every form it can take, including
the
rare
.BI \eu XXXX
spelling of a non-ASCII character.
.PP
Non-ASCII terms match. The logs are read as
.BR UTF-8 ,
never as latin1 \(em decoding them as latin1 is measurably the fastest option
and
is rejected precisely because it would silently break a search for a macronised
word such as
.IR Māori ,
which is the text this estate most cares about getting right.
.PP
It honours
.BR NO_COLOR ", " FORCE_COLOR " and " CCARCHIVE_DEST .
No environment variable is required.
.PP
The
.I .jsonl
schema is internal to Claude Code and can shift between releases. If a field
moves, the human view may need a nudge; the checked-in schema-drift contract
test is what catches such a change, failing loudly rather than mis-rendering in
silence.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ccarchive (1),
.BR ccrepo (1),
.BR jq (1)
.PP
The instruments overview:
.I instruments/README.md
in the atelier repository.
